--- Finding: a statement loop's item that is a list (a list of a list of lists),
--- compared with `eq`/`ne`, does not build compiled; the interpreter runs it.
---
--- crow run -i:  true
---               false
--- crow run:     error[E0277]: can't compare `&Vec<String>` with `Vec<String>`
---                 --> src/main.rs:39:43
---               error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---               refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
---               (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: true then false in both modes.
---
--- The same with ints (`[[1, 2], [3, 4]]`, item `eq [1, 2]`: E0277 can't compare
--- `&CrowTensor` with `CrowTensor`), and with the item on the right of a local
--- list (`ys = reverse[xs]` then `ys eq xs`: E0308 mismatched types, can't compare
--- `Vec<i64>` with `&Vec<i64>`). A list PARAMETER compared the same way builds
--- and agrees (`fn f[xs]` … `reverse[xs] eq xs`). Likely the root of the known
--- findings/kirkpatrick_seidel_algorithm-2.co (records: a loop's item borrowed
--- from what it walks, compared by value), met here with lists, whose item the
--- emitter also borrows. Found writing Tree from nesting levels (`back eq levels`,
--- the levels read back from the tree compared with the loop's input); worked
--- around by comparing inside a function that takes the levels as a parameter.
tests = [['a', 'b'], ['b', 'a']]
for xs in tests
    log[xs eq ['a', 'b']]
